<section class="phero"><div class="phero-media"><img src="/assets/img/photos/condo-building.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/condo-building-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/condo-building.webp 1200w" sizes="100vw" width="1200" height="900" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><i aria-hidden="true">/</i><a href="/appraisal-work/">Appraisal work</a><i aria-hidden="true">/</i><span aria-current="page">Commercial, Condo &amp; HOA Appraisal</span></nav><p class="kicker">Appraisal work</p><h1>Condo Association Insurance Appraisal in Maine</h1><p class="lede">Condo association insurance appraisal in Maine involves more moving parts than a house claim: a master policy, unit owner policies, bylaws and a board that answers to owners. We act as the association’s or business’s named appraiser and keep the file organized.</p><div class="btn-row"><a class="btn btn-coral" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="ic" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg><span>Call (844) 537-1427</span></a><a class="btn btn-line light" href="/reach-us/">Free claim review</a></div><dl class="spec"><div><dt>Typical client</dt><dd>Board or property manager</dd></div><div><dt>Typical issue</dt><dd>Master policy scope gaps</dd></div><div><dt>Our role</dt><dd>Named or neutral appraiser</dd></div></dl></div></section>
<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose"><h2>Who we work with</h2>
<p>We serve condominium associations and HOAs across southern Maine, ski-area associations near Sugarloaf and Sunday River, owners of brick mill conversions such as the Pepperell Mill in Biddeford and the Bates Mill in Lewiston, and small commercial owners from retail blocks in Bangor to metal farm buildings and potato storage houses in Aroostook County. Condo association insurance appraisal in Maine follows the same clause as a house claim, but the stakes, documentation and decision-making are different.</p>

<h2>Where master policy and commercial estimates fall short</h2>
<p>Large-loss estimates are often built in stages, and stages invite omissions. Common gaps include:</p>
<ul>
<li>Roof systems priced as small repairs when snow load or wind damage runs across a whole building.</li>
<li>Common elements, such as corridors, stairwells and mechanical rooms, left out of a scope written unit by unit.</li>
<li>Historic masonry, timber framing and large window systems in mill buildings priced as new construction.</li>
<li>General contractor overhead and profit, project management and temporary protection for occupied buildings.</li>
<li>Business income or loss of rents calculated on too short a restoration period for a Maine winter.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Boundaries between the master policy and unit owners</h2>
<p>Many Maine associations use bylaws and declarations that split responsibility: the master policy covers the structure and common elements, while unit owners insure their interiors and improvements. Disputes about where that line falls are coverage and governance questions, often worth an attorney’s review. Appraisal works best once everyone agrees which policy covers which items and the remaining argument is about dollars.</p>

<div class="note"><b class="h">Board decisions and records</b><p>Before demanding appraisal, a board should record its decision in minutes, confirm who has authority to sign on the association’s behalf, and keep owners informed. A clean decision trail avoids second-guessing later and helps the appraisal move faster.</p></div>

<h2>Documentation that helps commercial and association claims</h2>
<p>Bring the master policy and any endorsements, the declarations and bylaws, the reserve study, maintenance logs, prior roof or envelope reports, building plans if available, and every carrier estimate and letter. For business income, gather monthly financial statements from before and after the loss. Our <a href="/claim-math/appraisal-file-strength-test/">appraisal file strength test</a> is a quick way to see what is missing. If the dispute centers on a single large estimate, our <a href="/appraisal-work/carrier-estimate-line-review/">insurance estimate review</a> can come first to size the gap.</p>

<h3>Illustrative example</h3>
<p>Illustrative example: after a winter snow-load event, a 24-unit association in Scarborough receives $96,000 for roof and interior repairs. The association’s scope includes full membrane replacement on one building, common hallway repairs and winter protection, totaling $172,000. That $76,000 gap is a scope and pricing disagreement that appraisal can address. The figures are invented for illustration.</p>

<h2>Large-loss appraisal and the umpire</h2>
<p>On big files, the umpire matters. We look for umpires with real commercial estimating experience and avoid anyone with conflicts on either side. If you are a carrier or a policyholder looking for a neutral rather than an advocate, see our <a href="/appraisal-work/umpire-neutral-panel-services/">umpire and neutral services</a>.</p>

<h2>When to involve counsel</h2>
<p>Commercial and association files also tend to involve more people: property managers, lenders, contractors and sometimes public adjusters already on the claim. We coordinate with whoever is involved so the appraisal file stays consistent and nobody duplicates work.</p>
<p>If the carrier has denied part of the claim, raised a vacancy or protective safeguards condition, or disputed whether a policy covers a given element, involve a Maine attorney. The same applies to any question about policy deadlines. Appraisal decides the amount; lawyers handle coverage.</p>
<h2>Questions about commercial, condo &amp; hoa appraisal</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Can a condo board demand appraisal without a vote of all owners?</summary><p>That depends on the association’s declaration and bylaws. Most boards act through a recorded board vote, but confirm authority with the association’s attorney.</p></details><details><summary>Does condo association insurance appraisal in Maine cover individual units?</summary><p>It covers whatever the master policy insures. Unit interiors that fall under owners’ own policies are usually handled as separate claims with those carriers.</p></details><details><summary>Can business income be part of an appraisal?</summary><p>Often yes, if the policy’s appraisal clause applies to the full amount of loss. It requires solid financial records and a realistic restoration timeline.</p></details><details><summary>Do you handle mill buildings and historic structures?</summary><p>Yes. Brick mill conversions, timber-framed buildings and older masonry need specialized pricing, and we build that into the scope.</p></details></div>
